## Enabling an Inverted Index

There are two ways to enable an inverted index on a column in Pinot, depending on whether you're configuring a new table or updating an existing one.

### For New Tables (Recommended)

Use the `fieldConfigList` approach in your table configuration [1]:

```json
{
  "fieldConfigList": [
    {
      "name": "playerName",
      "indexes": {
        "inverted": {}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

If the column uses a RAW forward index, Pinot automatically materializes a standalone dictionary for the inverted index — you don't need separate dictionary configuration [1].

### For Existing Tables

To add an inverted index to existing segments [4]:

1. Update the table configuration by adding the column to `tableIndexConfig` → `invertedIndexColumns`:

```json
{
  "tableIndexConfig": {
    "invertedIndexColumns": ["playerName"]
  }
}
```

Use the Pinot Swagger API at `http://localhost:9000/help#!/Table/updateTableConfig` to apply this change [4].

2. Trigger a reload of all segments using `http://localhost:9000/help#!/Segment/reloadAllSegments` [4].

3. Verify the index was applied by querying the segment metadata API at `http://localhost:9000/help#/Segment/getServerMetadata` and checking that `"inverted-index": "YES"` appears in the output [4].

## How It Works

When an inverted index is enabled on an unsorted column, Pinot maintains a mapping from each value to a bitmap of document IDs, making value lookups constant time O(1) [3].

---

**Sources**

1. build-with-pinot/indexing/inverted-index.md — Inverted index > Bitmap inverted index > Configuration
3. build-with-pinot/indexing/inverted-index.md — Inverted index > Bitmap inverted index
4. operate-pinot/troubleshooting/ingestion-faq.md — Ingestion FAQ > Indexing > How to apply an inverted index to existing segments?

